            <div class="step2-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Unique Contribution</h2>
                <p>In a sentence, we could say that the Explorer archetype is the one archetype who can make us stop and take an in-depth look at our surroundings.</p>
                <p>Explorers contribute to the world by advocating freedom and believe that in attaining freedom that they can create paradise.  </p>
                <p>The Explorer takes the importance of freedom and transforms it by inspiring others - with the goal of creating paradise,  so that we can all live in paradise and so we can all become liberated.  Literally.</p>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/mandala-1.png">
                <p>The Explorer Archetype is one of the three archetypes who focus on creating paradise.  The Explorer does so through attaining ‘freedom,’ whereas the Sage focuses on using ‘understanding’ to bring about paradise, and then the Innocent Archetype creates their notion of paradise by promoting safety in everything they do.</p>
                <p><strong>The Explorer and The Innocent Are Total Opposites</strong></p>
                <p>It seems ironic to think that safety brings a sense of paradise - or at least it would to an Explorer.</p>
                <p>The Explorer's approach to attaining paradise is the total opposite to the Innocent’s; Explorers like to push the boundaries around them and relish having the freedom to physically explore uncharted waters it’s the Explorers idea of paradise.</p>
                <p><strong>Even Explorers Secretly Rely on Limitations</strong></p>
                <p>But what the Explorer hasn’t realized is that even though they might push the experiences they have to the edge of their limitations physically, they probably only do so with a deep inside knowledge that there is a high probability that they’ll be safe.</p>
                <p>This means that such efforts of exploration do come with limitations (something that the Explorer rebels against).</p>
                <p>Explorers also only usually explore physically such as through travel or sporty adventures - they don’t often explore the depths of their soul or through studying.</p>
                <p><strong>The Need to Respect the Value of Order</strong></p>
                <p>It’s this need for safety that the Explorer needs to ‘understand’ and appreciate.</p>
                <p>In doing so, when they bring freedom into the picture as a way of inspiring others and creating paradise, they do so by recognizing and respecting the value of order and structure. </p>
                <p>For if we create freedom without structure, it will only lead to a chaotic world!</p>
                <p>The push and pull between freedom and limitation is the perfect example of irony at its best!  And it’s the Explorer's entire experience in life.</p>
                <p>It’s this order and structure that keeps Explorers safe on their adventures and provides the platform for freedom that the Explorer archetype relishes so much! </p>
