            <div class="step5-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Strengths</h2>
                <p><strong>Open To Anything New</strong></p>
                <p>Other than commitment, the Explorer is open to experiencing anything new, especially if they are going to find a new way to perceive or experience the world. In fact; the newer experiences, the better for the Explorer.</p>
                <p>Such curiosity and the love of adventure makes the Explorer interesting to others and interested in their experiences and surroundings more than most other archetypes.</p>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/mandala-2.png">
                <p><strong>Generally Healthy Because of Their Active Lifestyle</strong></p>
                <p>Most Explorer Archetypes have an active lifestyle.  Which means that they are also usually healthy - although they might have to consider the risks that they might place on their health or well-being through the activities they participate in.</p>
                <p>For example, they are adept at protecting themselves against illnesses that can occur when traveling overseas, food poisoning or even harm through accidents or encounters as they experience life. </p>
                <p><strong>Sets High Sights And Has Every Intention Of Achieving Them</strong></p>
                <p>Setting and achieving goals is one skill that the Explorer has that could help them find the balance in their life that they probably need but also perhaps don’t concentrate on. </p>
                <p>It’s a great skill to be able to set, plan, and achieve the goals you create for yourself, something that the explorer does with ease. But most of the time, they don’t enjoy goal setting when they have to do so out of obligation.</p>
                <p>However, they could probably use this skill in many different ways to enhance their experience of life further if they decided that it would be good for them to do so.</p>
                <p><strong>Make Great Company</strong></p>
                <p>Explorers always have an exciting and insightful tale to tell, and they love to try new things which makes them fantastic company - unless you are the sort who likes to enjoy comfortable ritual nights in of course.</p>
                <p>Expect the Explorer to run for the hills if you want them to commit to too many of those!</p>
                <p><strong>Enjoys Learning About The World And Its Various Cultures</strong></p>
                <p>Explorers are people-oriented, and they will enjoy interacting with and learning about people from all walks of life.  They will express compassion, interest, and inspiration as they interact with the people they meet and may even bring some of the aspects of the cultures they’ve learned about into their own life. </p>
                <p>The problem is though is that this experience is limited to the Explorers own emotional and personal depths.</p>
                <p>If only they’d take the time to explore those depths so that they could really taste the cultures of the world!</p>
                <p><strong>Extremely Curious And Wants To See Everything That He Or She Hasn't Seen</strong></p>
                <p>Explorers are very inspiring in the way that they just want to explore everything that they have never explored before.  It doesn’t matter what it is, but if it’s new to them, then they’ll probably want to explore it. </p>
                
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Weaknesses</h2>
                <p><strong>Find Commitment A Challenge</strong></p>
                <p>The thought of settling down permanently in any way is enough to send the Explorer running straight to the hills faster than a cheetah who has just spotted lunch!  But part of the experience of life, if you want to explore it fully, requires commitment and mundane tasks. </p>
                <p>If the Explorer doesn’t learn to do this, they are at risk of not being able to experience the things they want to experience or to enjoy the benefits of commitment which leads us nicely onto the next point.</p>
                <p><strong>Explorers Struggle To Create Deep Connections</strong></p>
                <p>It's tough for Explorers to enjoy deep connections with others, or a deep connection with anything (a job, career, pet, property, etc.).  Their fear of commitment and curiosity for anything new causes them to feel bored if they are in the same environment for too long.</p>
                <p>Such fear of commitment is unfortunate for the Explorer because even though Explorers love new things, they only get to taste one flavor of everything when they could have so many different new experiences of one thing multiple times. If only the Explorer could pay some attention to developing toleration for commitment and personal development.</p>
                <p><strong>Are Not Always Good At Committing To Developing Their Skills</strong></p>
                <p>There’s no question that an Explorer would excel at anything that they do, especially since they excel at attaining goals.  It’s just that they probably won’t and often don’t excel at anything other than exploring new things.</p>
                <p>If you are starting to notice a slightly biased pattern toward only one aspect of living here, then you’ve figured out the Explorer Archetype.  As you’ll see in the next sections, there are ways to balance this significant influence in an Explorers life out in a way that an Explorer would be able to appreciate and tolerate! </p>
                <p><strong>Too Dependent On Seeking Opportunities For Exploration</strong></p>
                <p>When an Explorer cannot explore, they can feel immense emptiness inside, which as we’ve already discovered can lead to a whole host of undesirable experiences such as depression or apathy.  The risk of apathy is caused by the bias we have mentioned above, which causes the Explorer to be too dependent on opportunities for adventure.  Any situation in life where something is too reliant on something else is always vulnerable to weakness.</p>
                <p>It's easy to address this vulnerability though - there’s more information below, and in the Premium Archetype Reports.</p>
