            <div class="step6-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Greatest Challenge</h2>
                <p>Being able to balance their distaste for commitment so that they can enhance their exploration of life and reach new realms is the Explorer’s greatest challenge. </p>
                <p>If the Explorer could also enhance their inability to connect on a deep level emotionally, then life is going to be a world of wonderful and deeply fulfilling experiences, which is a far cry from the low level, general or even superficial meaning and wisdom that most Explorers gain from the single-faceted experiences that they often enjoy.</p>
                <p>It’s ironic that Explorers love to explore new heights but will never usually explore past the external experiences they have.  They’ll never usually explore the deeper aspects of life such as internal, emotional, intimate or even creative aspects, even though they have the skills and ability to do so with more ease than other archetypes may have.</p>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/success.png">
                <p>If an Explorer could realize the value of diving deeper into the opportunities for adventure that exist within them, or are easier to attain, they can start to make moves toward balancing out themselves.  They’d soon learn that it doesn’t matter who you are, or how limited you are - there are infinite ways of enjoying an adventure or two. </p>
                <p>The Explorer's whole existence could be an adventure and a deep and fulfilling one too if they would only go and visit that part of their internal world.</p>
                <p>We’ve already highlighted throughout this report that the balance in the way that Explorers live their life is awry leaving them extremely vulnerable to experiencing an unfulfilling life (or life experiences).</p>
                <p>However, not many Explorer archetypes will stop to explore their ‘self’ without being prompted or inspired to do so.  So if you are reading this as an Explorer, please hear the message, for we are showing you a map to adventures that are beyond your wildest dreams, which you can easily reap the rewards from.</p>
                <p><strong>How A Explorer Archetype Can Master Their Greatest Challenge</strong></p>
                <p>There are quite a lot of simple ways that an Explorer archetype can master their greatest challenge, but it might not seem so simple to the Explorer.</p>
                <p>They would first need to realize the risk their lifestyle choices put on their wellbeing as a way to motivate them to start to want to work on healing their fear of commitment.</p>
                <p>When the Explorer is ready to start turning inwards, they need to understand why they might not have explored this aspect of themselves beforehand.  Also, they need to let go of some of the social conditioning and beliefs that they have about commitment so that they can find a way to make commitments in the future that are comfortable and sustainable for them.</p>
                <p>The funny thing is that an Explorer is capable of commitment.  So much so that they might be one of the best archetypes at commitment, because they are wholeheartedly committed to exploring places or new experiences. That type of commitment doesn’t scare them away - but what they don't often realize is that it also doesn’t entirely fulfill them either.</p>
                <p><strong>Futureproof Your Sense of Happiness and Fulfillment Without Compromising on Your Freedom</strong></p>
                <p>If the Explorer could commit to working on their personal development or (but ideally and) on their creativity; such as through drawing, art, or other types of creative outlets, they will bring more balance out into the Explorers life.</p>
                <p>It will also future-proof their sense of happiness and fulfillment without compromising on their freedom for many years to come, and the premium archetype reports are a great way to get started on their personal development. </p>
